## 7. Hardware integration and qualification profiles

### Universal hardware strategy

Use a hardware abstraction layer with device adapters and capability profiles. Separate the universal application, integrations, and installation-specific profiles. Target Windows audio APIs first for the initial setup; keep other OS/backend integrations as later adapters rather than assuming identical capability across APIs.

Classify every capability as:

1. **Controllable:** a supported API can change it.
2. **Observable:** a supported API can report it.
3. **Manually configured:** it can be represented, but the operator sets or confirms the physical state.
4. **Unsupported/unknown:** unavailable or not yet verified.

Profiles must include device IDs, driver/backend, input/output counts and names, formats, sample rates, buffer support, clock/synchronization support, hardware metering, remote control, DSP, and connection/status evidence. Partial and unknown capabilities must be represented explicitly.

### Basic compatibility fixture: MG16XU and Xonar DGX

Yamaha specifies the MG16XU USB interface as **2-in/2-out**, so the application must not create sixteen independent computer capture channels from the mixer’s sixteen analog input channels. Independent computer processing of a source such as bass requires an isolated physical send/output or an interface that exposes that source separately. The MG16XU also has physical output options that can be assessed for that purpose; the actual cable and bus assignment must be recorded in the installation profile. See [Yamaha MG16XU product specifications](https://usa.yamaha.com/business/audio/products/mixers/mg/specs.html) and the [Yamaha MG-series overview](https://usa.yamaha.com/products/contents/proaudio/musicianspa/products_analog_mixer.html).

ASUS documents the Xonar DGX line input as an analog recording input. Treat its exact driver formats, channel count, available capture APIs, simultaneous operation with the MG16XU, and playback path as items to discover and verify on the target computer, not as assumptions. See the [ASUS Xonar DGX user manual](https://dlcdnets.asus.com/pub/ASUS/Audio_Card/Xonar_DGX/E11106_Xonar_DGX_UM_V5_web.pdf).

This is one **basic compatibility fixture only**. A professional release needs separate qualification profiles for representative multichannel pro interfaces and, before claiming networked-audio support, actual Dante/AES67/Milan-capable endpoints and their clock/network behavior.

The basic fixture can support these alternatives:

- **Preferred simple path:** MG16XU USB capture/playback using only the channels and formats reported by the device/driver.
- **Alternative capture path:** a suitable MG16XU analog output into the Xonar DGX line input, if tested and configured.

Do not combine two devices carrying the same source unless intentional. Separate device clocks may drift; measure latency and drift, and avoid claiming synchronization from resampling alone. Prefer one stable device for the initial capture/playback path when practical.

