## 13. Acceptance and quality criteria

The platform is not complete merely because audio reaches an output. The following evidence is required before a production release:

### Audio and graph correctness

- Device loss/reconnect and unsupported format paths are handled safely.
- One input can branch to multiple outputs and one output can be traced to all contributors.
- Disconnected, muted, bypassed, inactive, and unknown states are accurately shown.
- Invalid channel mappings and unintended routing cycles are rejected.
- A failed graph/DSP change leaves the previous valid configuration active.
- UI navigation and AI/service outages do not interrupt the real-time engine.
- The declared 128-input/64-output/64-bus profile passes sustained-load tests at every sample rate it claims to support.
- A device/network clock fault is detected and shown; any bypass or standby path behaves as documented.

### Measurement correctness

- Each meter identifies graph point, channel layout, timestamp, graph version, and measurement source.
- Captured stereo does not imply unavailable per-channel hardware measurements.
- Measured latency, configured buffer latency, and unknown hardware latency are separate values.
- Round-trip latency is established by loopback measurement rather than inferred from buffer size alone.

### Processing and usability

- Bypass is a real DSP bypass; faders/knobs/switches reflect engine state.
- EQ and crossover graphs reflect their actual configured parameters.
- Live changes are validated, auditable, and reversible where appropriate.
- Sound-check tone/output actions clearly identify affected outputs and require authorization.
- Preset application previews its changes and reports unsupported parameters.

### Initial performance targets

Treat these as starting targets to measure on the actual hardware, not guarantees: stable real-time processing with no dropouts during defined stress runs; normal UI actions under 100 ms; typical signal trace under 250 ms; meter updates around 20–30 Hz; AI failure with no audio interruption; and recovery to the last known valid configuration where safe.

For every production profile, add a minimum 24-hour continuous-load soak with zero xruns, CPU headroom under worst-case DSP/routing load, and fault-injection runs for stream loss, driver reset, clock loss, network interruption (when applicable), and app/UI/AI failure. An isolated passing profile is evidence for that exact OS, driver, firmware, interface, sample rate, and graph size only.

